    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d624">

                <pb facs="0877" n="763" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg763"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">724.3415/1426: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Bolivia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Feely</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">La
                                Paz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-13T20:00:00-04:32">October 13, 1931—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:25 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>124. The Argentine Minister today informed the Minister of Foreign Affairs as to
                    the contents of the Department’s telegram to the Embassy in Buenos Aires,<anchor
                        n="95" xml:id="d624-f1"/> expressing its alarm at the possibility of
                    Bolivia’s withdrawal from the conference and at the danger of further military
                    activities in the Chaco. Similar but more definite and forcible instructions
                    have been received by the Chilean and Peruvian Ministers and will be
                    communicated to the Foreign Office tomorrow morning. The Brazilian Minister is
                    without advice from his Government.</p>
                <p>The Minister of Foreign Affairs informed me at 6 p.m. today that the final vote
                    in the Chamber of Deputies began at 5:30 p.m. and that he could assure me that
                    none of the resolutions to be voted involved <pb facs="0878" n="764"
                        xml:id="pg764"/> Bolivia’s withdrawal from the conference, but had to do
                    principally with the nature of the instructions to be given to the Bolivian
                    Delegation.</p>
                <p>Under the circumstances I have suggested to him that Bolivia do not request a
                    postponement even though Finot may not arrive in time for the first sessions,
                    and he promised to consider the suggestion.</p>
                <p>The vote in the Chamber of Deputies will probably continue until Thursday.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Feely</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
